            <ArticleTitle>Pelvic instability - Painful pelvic girdle in pregnancy.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>409-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Pelvic instability is a term describing a group of conditions causing pelvic girdle pain in pregnancy. Although a common problem in pregnancy, it is easily overlooked and sometimes dismissed as 'normal' by primary care professionals.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">This article outlines the clinical features, diagnosis and management of pelvic instability in pregnancy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DISCUSSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">General practitioners are in an ideal position to diagnose pelvic instability, implement treatment, and refer for further care to a physiotherapist with an interest in women's health. Use of a supportive belt or taping, appropriate analgesia, core stability and pelvic floor exercises, and avoiding heavy lifting and activities that exacerbate the pain are the mainstays of treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
